f 1028 #edb/ref 17592186105911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050998 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186105912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050998 #edb/ref 1031 "Equivalent Expressions With Fractions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050998 #edb/ref 1033 1297 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050998 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "782cde63-c3cf-5599-8f18-28a9d8b09d13" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048565 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186179815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186179816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1031 "Constructing Algebraic Expressions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1033 3721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7931e951-f0f8-5132-97e2-67fa64802a65" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105485 #edb/ref 1069 "e-1521" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105485 #edb/ref 1071 "Find an expression equivalent to $\\dfrac 27\\, {(y-3)}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105485 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "45b1ee7d-b7ce-5336-9d2a-1a5953310026" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105485 #edb/ref 1074 "Writing $y-3$ as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y-3}{1}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2}{7}(y - 3) &= \\frac{2}{7} \\cdot (y - 3) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{7} \\cdot \\frac{y - 3}{1} \\\\\n&= (2 \\cdot (y - 3))/(7 \\cdot 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2y - 6}{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105486 #edb/ref 1069 "e-1543" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105486 #edb/ref 1071 "Find an expression that's equivalent to $\\dfrac{2}3 a$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105486 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "53825e9f-1b2f-582e-ad91-50970995d523" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105486 #edb/ref 1074 "Writing $a$ as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{1}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2}{3}a &= \\frac{2}{3} \\cdot a \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3} \\cdot \\frac{a}{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 \\cdot a}{3 \\cdot 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2a}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expressions\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}a\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2a}{3}\n$$\n\nare equivalent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105487 #edb/ref 1069 "e-1544" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105487 #edb/ref 1071 "Find an expression equivalent to $\\dfrac{2(3-x)}{5}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105487 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0c24fff9-3d43-53f3-8c87-daacb675f802" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105487 #edb/ref 1074 "Pulling out the variable part, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2(3 - x))/(5) &= (2 \\cdot (3 - x))/(5 \\cdot 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3 - x}{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{5} \\cdot (3 - x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{5}(3 - x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105683 #edb/ref 1069 "e-832" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105683 #edb/ref 1071 "Find an expression equivalent to $-\\dfrac{2}9 (-z)$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105683 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9062b933-10d8-5f52-b41e-c528d35c9fe5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105683 #edb/ref 1074 "First, remember that the product of two negative numbers is a positive number. Thus,\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{2}9 (-z) = \\dfrac{2}{9} z\n$$\n\nNow, writing $z$ as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{z}{1}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2}{9}z &= \\frac{2}{9} \\cdot z \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{9} \\cdot \\frac{z}{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 \\cdot z}{9 \\cdot 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2z}{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expressions\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{2}9 (-z)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2z}{9}\n$$\n\nare equivalent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105835 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105835 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105835 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105835 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105835 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Equivalent Expressions with Negative Terms" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105835 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186105683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105835 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "cd86e42a-deb3-5f59-bbbc-f096a1f368aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105910 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105910 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105910 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105910 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Equivalent Expressions with Binomial Terms" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105910 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186105485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105910 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "796da155-1e8c-5f6c-896f-2dce9a678f5b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105911 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105911 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186051009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105911 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105911 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105911 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Equivalent Expressions with Positive Terms" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105911 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186105486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105911 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "afca9dea-4471-582f-b15f-a5899d3789aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105912 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105912 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105912 #edb/ref 1039 "Expressing a Fraction as a Product" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105912 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186105487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105912 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "7002c6fc-ae50-5b7f-995f-2147db19e9ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105969 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105969 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106790 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105969 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105969 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105969 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105969 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105969 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105969 #edb/ref 1045 "Equivalent Expressions With Fractions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105969 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186106789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105969 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050998 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105969 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "c2a49596-9588-55a0-8deb-bab9b956aeec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106789 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106789 #edb/ref 1061 2820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106789 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2c48316d-05f5-59b9-a77c-b685a5127ce2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106789 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106790 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106790 #edb/ref 1061 2824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106790 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4c17202e-e185-5578-bb4c-14c96ae01ec3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106790 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106791 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106791 #edb/ref 1061 2827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106791 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0b0436c3-5877-556a-ab04-3a933c1b4463" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106791 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106932 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106932 #edb/ref 1061 4768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106932 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "eb7fe3e9-40a1-55ab-81cb-45bd464a0081" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106932 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105910 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106933 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106790 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106933 #edb/ref 1061 4899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106933 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "12adceb3-fbee-5964-9a8f-987db7df7bee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106933 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106934 #edb/ref 1061 4900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106934 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "471f6117-285f-5918-b5e3-f3eee6a5c192" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106934 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107032 #edb/ref 1064 "Two expressions involving fractions are **equivalent** if they are the same.\n\nEven if two expressions initially appear different, they might still be equivalent. For example, the expressions\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}x\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nboth represent the same thing: \"half of $x$.\" Consequently, they are equivalent.\n\nWe can also see that these expressions are equivalent because\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}x\n$$\n\ncan be transformed into\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nWriting $x$ as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x}{1}\n$$\n\nwe can multiply\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac x 1\n$$\n\nusing fraction multiplication, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}x &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot x \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{x}{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 \\cdot x}{2 \\cdot 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{x}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107032 #edb/ref 1065 1366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107032 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a90bc179-e52a-5754-8b86-3729572a90d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107032 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107033 #edb/ref 1064 "The usual procedure also works in the reverse direction. For example, to find an equivalent expression for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2(y+1)}5\n$$\n\nwe can separate the variable part from the rest of the fraction, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2(y + 1))/(5) &= (2 \\cdot (y + 1))/(5 \\cdot 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{5} \\cdot ((y + 1))/(1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{5} \\cdot (y + 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{5}(y + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107033 #edb/ref 1065 1367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107033 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "094b5543-1d48-5d70-9fdd-ea1dc9829418" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107033 #edb/ref 1067 "Expressing a Fraction as a Product" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
